course: reflection
lesson: 25
title: Disappointed: when things don't go the way you hoped
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Disappointment is the drop you feel when what happens is smaller than what you hoped for: Leah asked for a dog every single day for two years, and on her birthday she got a hamster; in her room she thought: "A HAMSTER? I asked for a dog. Every single day for two years."
explain: Then a second feeling came on top: guilt for being ungrateful, because her parents had tried; for a week she ignored the hamster; after a few weeks she loved him and called him Pickle.
explain: What she understood: disappointment and gratefulness can sit side by side, and both are normal; talking to Mum about the dog dream, instead of pretending, let the big feeling shrink, and learning about hamsters made room for the small one she had.
explain: Disappointment comes in many shapes: a burnt cake, a band that plays too fast, a cancelled holiday, a drawing not chosen, a disallowed goal; it usually shrinks over days, and it shrinks faster when you name it, laugh where you can, and see what can still be done.
explain: The thoughts that come with it, like "we never do anything fun" or "the ref's blind", are usually bigger and harsher than the facts; the feeling is real, the "never" and "always" usually are not.
fact: Disappointment is the feeling when things turn out worse than you hoped; it is normal and usually fades over days or weeks.
fact: You can feel disappointed and grateful at the same time; feeling both does not make you a bad person.
fact: Talking about the hope that didn't come true often helps more than hiding it.
fact: Being compared with a brother or sister can hurt; "in her shadow" means feeling less noticed than someone else, and everyone is good at different things.
fact: Kitchen mistakes happen to everyone; turning a mistake into something funny or new can rescue it.
fact: Live shows often go wrong a little; audiences tend to remember the fun more than the mistakes.
fact: Plans change and money can be tight; fun nearby, like a day out or a picnic, can still be good.
fact: Art is partly a matter of taste, which means different people like different things; "subjective" means depending on the person.
fact: Not being chosen once does not mean you can't do something; practice and lessons often change the result next time.
fact: In science, experiments often fail the first time; explaining why it failed is real science too, called trial and error.
fact: Even top chess players, called grandmasters, lose games; going over a lost game is one of the best ways to improve.
fact: Sport is not always fair, and referees make mistakes; learning to let go matters more than shouting.
fact: Thoughts with "never" and "always" in them, like "we never do anything fun", are usually exaggerations of a real feeling.
exercise: Leah wanted a dog for two years and got a hamster. What did she feel? | Disappointed, then guilty for feeling ungrateful | Only happy, because any pet is the same | Only angry, and she stayed angry for years | Nothing, because she had forgotten about the dog
exercise: Can you feel disappointed and grateful at once? | Yes | No; you have to pick one | Only once you are grown up | Only on your birthday
exercise: What helped Leah love the hamster? | Talking about her dog dream and learning about hamsters | Ignoring the hamster for weeks until the dog feeling went away | Telling her parents they got it wrong | Swapping the hamster for a dog
exercise: The teacher tells Max, "Your sister was such a star." He thinks, "I'm the thick one." What is true? | Everyone is good at different things | His sister is better than him at everything | He should copy his sister to get noticed | Teachers only remember the clever child
exercise: What helped Max feel better? | His mum praising his art and putting it on the wall | Trying to copy his sister's homework so he got the same marks | Telling the teacher his sister is boring | Giving up on school completely
exercise: Grace's bake sale cake came out burnt. What did she do? | Turned it into a volcano cake | Threw it in the bin and cried all day | Hid it and didn't go to the bake sale | Blamed her mum for the oven
exercise: Finn's band played badly because the drummer went too fast. What helped? | Watching the video and laughing | Quitting the band forever | Blaming the drummer in front of everyone at school | Never watching the video
exercise: Isla's holiday was cancelled because of money. What helped? | A family day out nearby | Sulking for the whole summer | Saying she never has any fun | Asking friends to pay for one
exercise: Ethan's drawing wasn't picked for the school wall. What is true about art? | Different people like different things | The best drawing always wins, so his can't have been the best | His drawing must have been bad | Teachers can't judge art at all
exercise: What helped Ethan? | Putting the drawing on his own wall | Saying the other drawing was rubbish | Tearing his drawing up | Never drawing again
exercise: Zoe wasn't picked for the choir and thinks, "I'll never sing again." What happened next year? | She tried again and got in | She never sang again | She was picked without trying | She joined a different school's choir
exercise: Tom's experiment didn't work at the science fair. What made him feel better? | Explaining why it didn't work to the judge | Pretending to the judge that it had worked perfectly | Blaming the other children | Leaving the fair early
exercise: What is trial and error? | Trying things, seeing what fails, and learning from it | Being put on trial for making errors | Never trying anything at all, in case you make an error | Getting it right first time, every time
exercise: Lucy lost her chess match in two minutes. What helped? | Going over the game with her coach | Refusing to play chess ever again | Saying the other player must have cheated | Hiding her chess set
exercise: Ben's goal was disallowed and his team lost. What helped? | Talking it through with Dad and letting it go | Shouting at the ref after the match until he changed his mind | Refusing to play the next game | Kicking the ball out of the ground
test: Leah's mate goes, "a hamster?? that's so mid." ("Mid" means not great.) Leah feels sad again. What's the honest view? | It's okay to feel both and come to love him | Her mate is right; hamsters are worthless pets | Leah should give the hamster back right away | Leah is ungrateful and should feel ashamed forever
test: Max's gran from Cardiff says, "Don't be tamping, bach, you're lush at drawing." ("Tamping" means furious; "bach" means dear; "lush" means lovely, great.) What's she telling him? | He has his own talents, like drawing | He should be angry with his sister | He's bad at everything except drawing | He should stop drawing and study more
test: What would happen if Grace had thrown the cake away? | She would have missed the fun of the volcano that sold first | The bake sale would have been cancelled | Her next cake would come out burnt too, and the one after that | Nobody would have bought any cakes at all
test: Finn says, "We were awful. Everyone heard. I wanna crawl under a rock." What's usually true about school shows? | People remember the fun more than the mistakes | Everyone remembers every single wrong note forever | Bands that play badly are banned from shows | Nobody watches school shows anyway
test: Isla says, "We NEVER do anything fun. It's ALWAYS cancelled." What's wrong with that thought? | "Never" and "always" exaggerate a real disappointment | It's exactly right; her family never has fun | She should be cross with her parents for weeks | Holidays abroad are the only fun that counts
test: Ethan's classmate from Birmingham says, "Yow drawing were bostin', it's a fix!" ("Bostin'" means brilliant; "it's a fix" means it was rigged.) What's fairer? | Art is a matter of taste; it wasn't necessarily rigged | The teachers definitely cheated against Ethan | The other drawing was obviously rubbish and should never have been picked | Ethan should complain until it's changed
test: Zoe's choir teacher says, "Practice makes progress." What's the point? | With practice, she can improve and try again | Practice is pointless if you weren't picked | Only singers who are already perfect should bother to practise | She should give up and try a sport
test: In a cartoon, the scientist's experiment works first time, every time. What's real science like? | Experiments often fail first, and explaining why is part of it | Real experiments always work first time | Failed experiments mean the scientist is bad | Scientists hide failed experiments forever
test: Lucy's coach says, "Even grandmasters lose." (A grandmaster is a top chess player.) What helped Lucy want to play again? | Going over the lost game with her coach | Being told she'd win next time for sure | Playing only against much younger kids | Never thinking about the game again
test: Ben's mate from Boston goes, "That call was wicked unfair, the ref's blind." ("Wicked" in New England means very.) What helped Ben? | Talking it through with Dad and letting it go | Shouting at the ref even louder in the next game to make up for it | Refusing to play under that ref again | Staying angry for the rest of the season
test: Leah thinks: "I'm ungrateful for being sad." Which is true? | Disappointment and gratefulness can be felt at the same time | Being disappointed with a present means she is ungrateful | She should never feel disappointed about gifts | Only her parents are allowed to feel disappointed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises disappointment? | A normal response to an outcome falling short of one's hopes, which customarily diminishes | An abnormal condition requiring immediate medical attention | A permanent state persisting for the remainder of one's life | A feeling experienced solely in respect of birthday presents
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of a thought containing the words "never" and "always" during disappointment. | It commonly exaggerates a genuine feeling beyond the facts | It invariably constitutes an accurate statement of fact | It is a reliable prediction of future events | It indicates that the disappointment has concluded
exam: It is given that an experiment presented at a science fair failed to operate. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | An explanation of the failure constitutes valid scientific work | The experimenter is deemed to have no aptitude for science | Failed experiments are to be concealed from the judges | Scientific experiments invariably succeed at the first attempt
exam: In respect of the term "subjective" as applied to the judging of artwork, determine which description is correct. | Dependent upon the preferences of the individual judging | Determined by a fixed and universal rule that all judges must apply | Indicative of deliberate dishonesty by the judges | Applicable solely to works of science
